WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.936
Picture a restaurant with an odd rule.

2
00:00:04.936 --> 00:00:06.878
Every table keeps its own waiter.

3
00:00:06.878 --> 00:00:12.704
The waiter takes the order to the kitchen, then stands at the kitchen door until the food is ready.

4
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:14.303
It has 200 waiters.

5
00:00:14.303 --> 00:00:21.080
On a busy night, the 201st party waits outside, while nearly every waiter stands by that door.

6
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.748
That door has never had so many fans.

7
00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:27.131
So here's a question that sounds too simple to ask.

8
00:00:27.131 --> 00:00:29.737
A waiter at the door isn't doing any work.

9
00:00:29.737 --> 00:00:32.157
So what's the harm in letting him wait?

10
00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:37.809
Many Java servers work like that restaurant.

11
00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:39.442
Take a small online shop.

12
00:00:39.442 --> 00:00:48.983
To check out a basket, its program reads the request and asks the stock service, a program on another computer, if the items are in the warehouse.

13
00:00:48.983 --> 00:00:56.891
Then it asks the bank to take the payment, saves the order in its database, the program that keeps its data, and replies.

14
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.392
One checkout takes 200 milliseconds.

15
00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:02.496
Reading the request, 2.

16
00:01:02.496 --> 00:01:04.508
The stock service, 40.

17
00:01:04.508 --> 00:01:05.788
The bank, 120.

18
00:01:05.788 --> 00:01:07.343
The database, 35.

19
00:01:07.343 --> 00:01:08.532
The reply, 3.

20
00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:14.465
Only 5 of them run on the processor, the part of the computer that does the work.

21
00:01:14.465 --> 00:01:17.579
The other 195 are spent waiting for answers.

22
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:18.916
Now let's map it.

23
00:01:18.916 --> 00:01:20.491
A table is one checkout.

24
00:01:20.491 --> 00:01:24.363
A waiter is a thread, one path of work through the program.

25
00:01:24.363 --> 00:01:27.054
The kitchen door is waiting for the bank.

26
00:01:27.054 --> 00:01:33.289
The 200 waiters are a pool of 200 threads, taking checkouts from a waiting line called a queue.

27
00:01:33.289 --> 00:01:36.571
And the party outside is a checkout in that queue.

28
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.731
The thread stays with its checkout to the very end.

29
00:01:39.731 --> 00:01:43.640
While it waits, it's blocked: it can't go on until the answer comes.

30
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:45.689
And that answers our question.

31
00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:49.576
A blocked thread does no work, but no other checkout can use it.

32
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.633
Shrink it to one thread.

33
00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:55.910
It finishes a checkout every 0.2 seconds, so 5 a second.

34
00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.353
How many can 200 threads finish?

35
00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:02.350
1,000 a second.

36
00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:03.955
And not one more.

37
00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:06.458
Let's watch it.

38
00:02:06.458 --> 00:02:09.037
Here's the pool, one square per thread.

39
00:02:09.037 --> 00:02:11.417
A square fills as its checkout runs.

40
00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:17.623
At 800 checkouts a second, 160 threads are busy and 40 sit idle.

41
00:02:17.623 --> 00:02:19.019
So far so good.

42
00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:23.668
Now traffic rises to 1,500 checkouts a second.

43
00:02:23.668 --> 00:02:24.912
What happens?

44
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.647
The idle threads fill at once.

45
00:02:29.647 --> 00:02:35.300
The pool still finishes 1,000 a second, so 500 more each second join the queue.

46
00:02:35.567 --> 00:02:42.827
After ten seconds, 5,000 are waiting, and a new checkout waits 5 seconds before a thread even picks it up.

47
00:02:43.067 --> 00:02:45.663
So the server must be working flat out, right?

48
00:02:45.663 --> 00:02:48.541
Let's freeze it and count the threads running code.

49
00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:49.413
About 5.

50
00:02:49.413 --> 00:02:53.939
Each one spends a fortieth of its time on the processor.

51
00:02:53.939 --> 00:02:56.040
The other 195 are blocked.

52
00:02:56.267 --> 00:03:02.061
The server has 16 cores, the parts of the processor that each run one thread at a time.

53
00:03:02.061 --> 00:03:04.259
So 11 of them have nothing to do.

54
00:03:04.467 --> 00:03:06.441
A slow shop on a bored machine.

55
00:03:06.441 --> 00:03:10.136
A faster processor would be a faster car in a traffic jam.

56
00:03:10.136 --> 00:03:13.831
We run out of threads long before we run out of processor.

57
00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:19.933
Now say the bank has a slow afternoon, and takes 1.2 seconds to answer.

58
00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:26.996
A checkout now takes 1.28 seconds, and the same 200 threads finish only 156 a second.

59
00:03:27.233 --> 00:03:29.447
We didn't change a line of our code.

60
00:03:29.447 --> 00:03:32.213
The bank had a slow afternoon, and so did we.

61
00:03:32.213 --> 00:03:35.595
So measure a slow service on a bad day, not a good one.

62
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.858
Does it hold on a real machine?

63
00:03:38.858 --> 00:03:45.923
The lesson's WallDemo runs 2,000 fake checkouts that each sleep 100 milliseconds, on 100 threads.

64
00:03:45.923 --> 00:03:47.671
How long should it take?

65
00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:53.660
That's 1,000 a second, so about 2 seconds.

66
00:03:53.660 --> 00:04:01.011
A busy 4-core machine took 2,229 milliseconds: the prediction, plus a little start-up time.

67
00:04:01.833 --> 00:04:04.258
This arithmetic is called Little's law.

68
00:04:04.258 --> 00:04:10.103
How many are inside at once equals how many arrive each second, times how long each one stays.

69
00:04:10.333 --> 00:04:17.048
Turned around, threads divided by the time each one is held is the most a pool can finish.

70
00:04:17.048 --> 00:04:20.779
That's 1,000 a second, or 156 on a slow afternoon.

71
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.915
And the style is called thread-per-request: one thread for a request's whole life, waits included.

72
00:04:26.915 --> 00:04:28.907
The restaurant's personal waiter.

73
00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:31.307
So why not hire more waiters?

74
00:04:31.307 --> 00:04:32.731
Say, 2,000 threads?

75
00:04:32.933 --> 00:04:40.480
Each one is a platform thread, backed by one thread of the operating system, the program that runs all the others.

76
00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:48.755
Starting one is a request to the operating system, and each one reserves 1 MB for its stack, the memory for its method calls.

77
00:04:48.967 --> 00:04:54.596
That's the waiter's contract and his locker, paid even for one who just stands at the door.

78
00:04:54.596 --> 00:04:58.431
So one Java program runs thousands of them, but not a million.

79
00:04:59.233 --> 00:05:02.176
And here the restaurant picture breaks, against us.

80
00:05:02.176 --> 00:05:05.406
A real waiter at the door could at least refill a glass.

81
00:05:05.406 --> 00:05:07.829
A blocked thread can't do anything at all.

82
00:05:08.533 --> 00:05:13.789
Here's the checkout in Java and Kotlin: three plain calls on a pool of 200.

83
00:05:13.789 --> 00:05:17.784
It passes every test, and fails only at the busiest hour.

84
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.125
So, what's the harm in a waiting thread?

85
00:05:21.125 --> 00:05:23.903
It does no work, but nobody else can use it.

86
00:05:23.903 --> 00:05:29.521
Threads divided by the time each one is held is your ceiling, however idle the processor.

87
00:05:29.733 --> 00:05:30.562
Quick check.

88
00:05:30.562 --> 00:05:34.084
200 threads, and each checkout now takes 2 seconds.

89
00:05:34.084 --> 00:05:36.708
How many a second can the shop finish?

90
00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:40.147
100.

91
00:05:40.147 --> 00:05:43.831
200 threads divided by 2 seconds.
